{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgNKXr7YZznWEUqnAGWB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the date bug in `journal.py`. All 14 tests pass (the 4 existing ones plus 10 new ones), and 9 of the new tests fail on the old code. I haven't committed anything.\n\n**What was wrong (both confirmed before the fix):**\n1. **The stored date was the UTC date, not the author's.** `publish` stamped `now` in UTC. A post written at 23:30 on Jul 31 in Buenos Aires was saved as `2026-08-01`. Authors east of UTC got the same problem in the early morning: 00:30 on Aug 1 in Tokyo was saved as Jul 31.\n2. **The card converted the date into the viewer's time zone.** `display_date` set the date to noon UTC and converted it to the viewer's zone. The code comment says this can never change the day, but it does for zones more than 12 hours from UTC: a viewer in `Pacific/Kiritimati` (UTC+14) saw that same post as `2026-08-02`.\n\n**What I changed, following the guide:**\n- **The date is the author's own date and is never converted.** If the client sends `entry[\"date\"]` (the author's local `YYYY-MM-DD`), it's stored exactly as sent; a value in the wrong format is rejected. If no date is sent, it's worked out from the server clock in the author's reported `client_tz`, never in UTC.\n- **`display_date` now just returns the stored date.** It still accepts `viewer_tz` so existing callers don't break, but ignores it. Every viewer sees the date the author wrote.\n- **Each post gets a `received_at` UTC timestamp, used only for ordering.** `created_at` is kept with the same value for existing users of that field.\n- **`feed()` sorts by `received_at`, newest first, then by date.** It used to sort by date and then author name, so posts from the same day came out in alphabetical order rather than the order they were posted.\n\n**The guide's verification scenario is now a test:** authors in Tokyo and Buenos Aires each publish at 23:30 on Jul 31, local time. Both posts show `2026-07-31`, and the feed lists them in the order they were submitted.\n\n**Things you should know:**\n- **The frontend still needs to send `date`.** The guide wants the date captured on the author's device. Until the client sends it, the backend uses the time zone the browser reports, which gives the right date as long as that zone is correct.\n- **An unknown `client_tz` is now an error.** If a post has no `date` and its `client_tz` isn't a valid zone name, `publish` raises `ValueError`. Before, any value was accepted, so the API handler should turn this error into a 400 response.\n- **The client's date isn't range-checked.** Back-dating entries seemed legitimate for a journal. If you'd rather not allow it, adding a ±1 day limit around the server's date is a small change."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":843,"cache_read_input_tokens":26037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":843},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0f871a9c-7509-49ec-bfe5-cbd659b3cb1c","uuid":"06bea198-6960-449d-a169-906aa86fd702","timestamp":"2026-10-04T01:58:20.732Z","request_id":"req_011CfgNKXYWE5txMiYdn5ohW"}
